{
  "id": "12-guard-what-enters",
  "title": "Guard What Enters",
  "tenet": "Let the Word shape the inner life",
  "durationMs": 82224,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/12-guard-what-enters.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/12-guard-what-enters.json",
  "text": "What words do you find yourself repeating when no one is speaking to you?\n\nA girl walked past the same bright advertisement each day. Its promise was simple: you are worth more when others desire you. At first she barely noticed it. Soon she heard its message in her own thoughts. Her grandmother did not tell her to pretend she had never seen it. She asked what other voice the girl wanted to hear. Together they read a passage about being loved by God, and the girl chose a different route home. She returned to that passage whenever the old message surfaced.\n\nScripture speaks of the heart as our inner life of thought, desire, and will. The modern word subconscious can be a helpful comparison for learned patterns, but it is not a translation of the biblical heart. Proverbs warns about a person's hidden motives; it does not promise that thoughts alone create reality. Jesus says to take care what we hear, and Paul says faith comes through hearing Christ's word. New birth in Christ is real, and our habits and desires still need patient renewal. Repeated attention can strengthen a habit, for good or harm, but no prayer mechanically erases a memory. Notice what you watch, hear, and rehearse. Choose one time today to hear Scripture instead. It is never too late to begin. Ask God to grow a truer desire, then practice the good it calls for.",
